Section A — Conceptual answers
S1. Database constraints and data integrity
Constraints help maintain accurate and consistent data.
1.	PRIMARY KEY: restaurant_id in the restaurants table uniquely identifies every restaurant. Without it, duplicate or ambiguous restaurant records could occur.
2.	NOT NULL: name in the restaurants table must have a value. Without this constraint, a restaurant could be stored without a name.
3.	FOREIGN KEY: restaurant_id in menu_items references restaurants.restaurant_id. Without it, menu items could refer to restaurants that do not exist, creating orphan records.
S2. Importance of the WHERE clause
The WHERE clause restricts which records are modified or deleted.
UPDATE orders
SET status = 'Cancelled'
WHERE order_date < NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY
  AND status = 'Pending';
DELETE FROM menu_items
WHERE availability = 'Unavailable'
  AND restaurant_id IN (
      SELECT restaurant_id
      FROM restaurants
      WHERE status = 'Closed'
  );
Without a proper WHERE clause, an UPDATE could change every order, and a DELETE could remove every menu item. Always verify the target records with a SELECT before running destructive statements.
Assumption: The examples use status and availability columns as described by the scenario.
S3. Aggregate revenue reporting
Use SUM(), AVG(), COUNT(), GROUP BY, and HAVING.
SELECT
    restaurant_id,
    SUM(total_amount) AS total_revenue,
    AVG(total_amount) AS average_order_value,
    COUNT(order_id) AS total_orders
FROM orders
WHERE YEAR(order_date) = YEAR(CURDATE())
  AND MONTH(order_date) = MONTH(CURDATE())
GROUP BY restaurant_id
HAVING COUNT(order_id) > 20;
WHERE filters individual rows before grouping. HAVING filters groups after aggregate calculations, which is why it is needed for COUNT(order_id) > 20.
S4. INNER JOIN versus LEFT JOIN
Use a LEFT JOIN because the page must display every order, even if its restaurant record no longer exists.
SELECT
    o.order_id,
    r.name AS restaurant_name,
    o.total_amount
FROM orders o
LEFT JOIN restaurants r
    ON o.restaurant_id = r.restaurant_id;
•	INNER JOIN returns only orders with matching restaurant records.
•	LEFT JOIN returns every order. If no restaurant matches, restaurant_name is NULL.
S5. Benefits and limitations of a VIEW
A view named top_restaurants can present restaurant names, total orders, and average ratings without exposing unnecessary ID columns.
Benefits:
•	Simplifies complex queries and improves consistency.
•	Restricts access to selected columns, helping protect underlying data.
Two limitations:
1.	A view does not automatically store a separate copy of its data; results generally reflect the underlying tables.
2.	Complex views involving aggregation and joins may not support direct INSERT, UPDATE, or DELETE operations.
S6. Transactions and error handling
A transaction should wrap the wallet deduction and payment-record insertion together.
•	COMMIT permanently saves both operations.
•	ROLLBACK undoes the transaction if either operation fails.
•	SAVEPOINT creates a marker to which the transaction can partially roll back.


